{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Do not shed blood.</b> He said to them: See, I was bearing with you when you plotted to kill him through your schemes, for I too hated him and wanted him to die at the hands of others, but you — do not shed blood with your own hands, far be it from you. And Reuben's intention in all this was to rescue him, to return him to his father. And Scripture recounted what Reuben said to them and that they listened to him, but he had said other things to them earlier that they did not accept from him, as he later said to them (Genesis 42:22), \"Did I not speak to you, saying, Do not sin against the child, and you did not listen?\" And when he saw that they would not listen to leave him alone, he said to them: If so, do not shed blood with your own hands. And he did not say \"his blood,\" for he presented himself as though he would not say so out of love for him, but only that they should not be shedding blood, teaching them that the punishment for one who causes death is not like the punishment for one who sheds blood with his own hands. And the sense of \"this pit that is in the wilderness\" is to say: See, this pit is deep and he cannot get out of it, and it is in the wilderness, so if he cries out there is none to save him, for no one passes by it. And Scripture relates that it was empty and had no water in it, because if it had had water in it, they would not have drowned him in it, since they had already refrained from shedding his blood. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 37:24): From the implication of what is stated, \"the pit was empty,\" I do not know that it had no water — so what does Scripture teach by saying \"there was no water in it\"? There was no water in it, but there were snakes and scorpions in it — this is the language of Rashi, from the words of our Sages (Shabbat 22a). And if so, there were snakes and scorpions in the holes of the pit, or it was deep and they did not know about them, for had they seen them and they did not harm Joseph, the matter would have been clear to them that a great miracle had been performed for him and that he was utterly righteous, and they would have known that his merit would save him from all harm — so how could they lay hands on the anointed of HaShem, in whom He delights and whom He saves, in the manner stated (Daniel 6:23), \"My Elohim sent His angel and shut the lions' mouths, and they did not harm me, for innocence was found in me before Him\" — but they did not know of the matter. And according to the plain sense, one would read \"the pit was empty, there was no water in it\" as absolute, for even if it had had a little water, it would still be called empty, and likewise \"for you are dead and shall not live\" (II Kings 20:1), absolute in any case — and all is elucidated and reinforced."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:20.879910440Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}